21.5  Why a tool may not watch the kernel

TL;DR
  - Profilers and eBPF tools (perf, bpftrace, Omniscient) ask the kernel to
    watch other code. A few settings decide whether an ordinary user may.
  - kernel.perf_event_paranoid limits perf events: at 2, the default on most
    distros, you can profile your own programs in user space only.
  - CAP_PERFMON and CAP_BPF lift those limits for one program, without running
    it as root.
  - Lockdown, a missing BTF file or a small locked-memory limit can still stop a
    tool that holds both.

      sysctl kernel.perf_event_paranoid kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled
      cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown        # the mode in brackets
      ls -l /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux            # present: portable eBPF can load
      sudo setcap cap_perfmon,cap_bpf+ep ./mytool   # getcap to check

On your machine
  Perf      perf_event_paranoid 2: your own processes only, in user space
  BPF       unprivileged eBPF refused without CAP_BPF (2)
  Addresses kernel addresses hidden without CAP_SYSLOG (kptr_restrict 1)
  Lockdown  none: off
  BTF       present: portable eBPF programs (CO-RE) can load
  Memlock   8.0 MiB locked per process

Pitfalls
  - A file capability is lost when the file changes: rebuilding the binary drops
    it, so set it again after every build.
  - Debian and Ubuntu patch the kernel so that a paranoid level of 3 or 4
    refuses perf to anyone without CAP_PERFMON, even on their own programs.
  - On kernels before 5.11, eBPF maps are charged to the locked-memory limit:
    with the old 64 KiB default, loading a map fails with "operation not
    permitted", which looks like a missing permission.
  - Lowering perf_event_paranoid affects every user of the machine. A capability
    on one binary is the narrower choice.

Deep dive: 3 questions. Read them with atfq 21.5 --why

Related
  Commands  sysctl, setcap, getcap, ulimit
  Paths     /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid, /sys/kernel/security/lockdown,
            /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux
  See also  atfq 21.1 sysctl
  See also  atfq 15.4 ACLs and capabilities
  See also  atfq 21.4 syscalls and strace
  See also  atfq 8.4 Too many open files: limits on processes
